is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: r1
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: The OSPF adjacency on R1’s WAN-facing internal link to the core (OSPF peer 10.0.0.77).

Mechanism: When the OSPF neighbor to one core switch drops, that core (CSW1) withdraws the default route it learns from R1. Upstream distribution switches continue to ECMP-hash traffic toward both cores, so any new flows hashed toward CSW1 see no working default and stall (SYN timeouts). Flows hashed toward CSW2 still work, which makes connectivity appear flaky while established flows (already hashed) often continue.

Localization: On R1, the neighbor 10.0.0.77 (CSW1, loopback shown on CSW1 as 10.0.0.77/32) over interface eth0 (10.0.0.33/30 to CSW1 10.0.0.34) recently reset. Evidence:
- R1 “show ip ospf neighbor detail” shows neighbor 10.0.0.77 on eth0 with a “Most recent state change” about 2 minutes ago and “State is Full/DR,” indicating a recent adjacency down/up on that link.
- CSW1 “show ip ospf neighbor” shows its adjacency to R1 (10.0.0.76) with a similar short uptime (~2 minutes), corroborating the flap specifically on the R1–CSW1 adjacency.
- R1 dmesg shows rapid bridge topology-change events around the same time, consistent with transient L2 disruption that can momentarily interrupt OSPF hellos.

Root cause: A transient L2/bridging event on R1 caused the OSPF adjacency to CSW1 (10.0.0.77) on eth0 to drop and recover.

Corrective fix: Stabilize the L2 domain on R1’s internal bridge (avoid spanning-tree churn on OSPF links), or remove bridging from OSPF interfaces. Optionally enable BFD for faster failover and ensure only routed point-to-point links are used for OSPF toward the cores. === END ===